        "description": "Christian apostle and theologian, author of several epistles.",
        "note": "While 'Paulo' and 'Paul' appear, the context of being an 'authority on medicine' in 'Coloquios da Orta' and 'Polyanthea Medicinal' points strongly to the author Garcia de Orta and a general reference to influential figures, but the biblical Paul is the most prominent figure referred to as Paul in historical texts. The diverse contexts suggest it could refer to either the apostle in theological discussions or as a general reference to a learned individual whose name might appear in compendia.",
        "wikidata_id": "Q3947759",
        "wikidata_description": "human settlement in Sassari, Province of Sassari, Sardinia, Italy",
        "wikipedia_extract": "Paul (born Saul of Tarsus; c. 5 – c. 64/65 AD), commonly known as Paul the Apostle or Saint Paul, was a Christian apostle who spread the teachings of Jesus in the first-century AD. For his contributions towards the New Testament, he is generally regarded as one of the most important figures of the Apostolic Age, and he also founded several Christian communities in Asia Minor and Europe from the mid-40s to the mid-50s AD. The main sources of information on Paul's life and works are Paul's letters and the Acts of the Apostles in the New Testament. Approximately half of its content documents his travels, preaching, and miracles. Paul was not one of the Twelve Apostles, and he did not know Jesus during his lifetime. Nonetheless, Paul was a contemporary of Jesus and personally knew eyewitnesses of Jesus such as his closest disciples (Peter and John) and brother James since the mid 30s AD, within a few years of the crucifixion (ca. 30–33 AD)."
