        "confidence": "high",
        "type": "substance",
        "note": "The term 'Ambar' or 'âmbar' in early modern Portuguese and Spanish texts most commonly refers to ambergris, a waxy substance produced in the digestive system of sperm whales, used in perfumery and medicine. While 'amber' (fossilized resin) also exists, its uses and contexts in early modern medicinal texts point more strongly to ambergris.",
        "wikidata_id": "Q188774",
        "wikidata_description": "substance produced in the digestive system of sperm whales",
        "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambergris",
        "wikipedia_extract": "Ambergris ( or ; Latin: ambra grisea; Old French: ambre gris), ambergrease, or grey amber is a solid, waxy, flammable substance of a dull grey or blackish colour produced in the digestive system of sperm whales. Freshly produced ambergris has a marine, fecal odor. It acquires a sweet, earthy scent as it ages, commonly likened to the fragrance of isopropyl alcohol without the vaporous chemical astringency. Ambergris has been highly valued by perfume makers as a fixative that allows the scent to last much longer, although it has been mostly replaced by synthetic ambroxide. It is sometimes used in cooking. Dogs are attracted to the smell of ambergris and are sometimes used by ambergris searchers."
