        "wikipedia_extract": "A phenomenon  (pl. phenomena), sometimes spelled phaenomenon, is an observable event. The term came into its modern philosophical usage through Immanuel Kant, who contrasted it with the noumenon, which cannot be directly observed. Kant was heavily influenced by Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in this part of his philosophy, in which phenomenon and noumenon serve as interrelated technical terms. Far predating this, the ancient Greek Pyrrhonist philosopher Sextus Empiricus also used phenomenon and noumenon as interrelated technical terms. In popular usage, a phenomenon often refers to an extraordinary, unusual, or notable event. According to the Dictionary of Visual Discourse:In ordinary language 'phenomenon/phenomena' refer to any noteworthy occurrence and investigation, typically an untoward or unusual event, person or fact that is of special significance or otherwise notable."
