        "wikidata_description": "island region in the Caribbean",
        "wikipedia_extract": "The West Indies are an island subregion of the Americas, surrounded by the North Atlantic Ocean and the Caribbean Sea, which comprises 13 independent island countries and 18 dependencies in three archipelagos: the Greater Antilles, the Lesser Antilles, and the Lucayan Archipelago. The subregion includes all the islands in the Antilles, in addition to The Bahamas and the Turks and Caicos Islands, which are in the North Atlantic Ocean. The term is often interchangeable with \"Caribbean\", although the latter may also include coastal regions of Central and South American mainland nations. The English term Indie is derived from the Classical Latin India, a reference to the territories in South Asia adjacent and east to the Indus River. India is borrowed from Ancient Greek Ἰνδία, which is borrowed from Old Persian Hindush (an eastern province of the Achaemenid Empire), whose cognate is Sanskrit Sindhu, which means 'river'. The ancient Greeks referred to the Indians as Indoi (Ἰνδοί), lit. 'people of the Indus'."
