        "wikipedia_extract": "In statistical thermodynamics, thermodynamic beta, also known as coldness, is the reciprocal of the thermodynamic temperature of a system: {\\displaystyle \\beta \\equiv {\\frac {1}{k_{\\rm {B}}T}}} (where T is the temperature and kB is Boltzmann constant). Thermodynamic beta has units reciprocal to that of energy (in SI units, reciprocal joules, {\\displaystyle [\\beta ]={\\textrm {J}}^{-1}} ). In non-thermal units, it can also be measured in byte per joule, or more conveniently, gigabyte per nanojoule; 1 K−1 is equivalent to about 13,062 gigabytes per nanojoule; at room temperature: T = 300K, β ≈ 44 GB/nJ ≈ 39 eV−1 ≈ 2.4×1020 J−1. The conversion factor is 1 GB/nJ = Thermodynamic beta is essentially the connection between the information theory and statistical mechanics interpretation of a physical system through its entropy and the thermodynamics associated with its energy. It expresses the response of entropy to an increase in energy. If a small amount of energy is added to the system, then β describes the amount the system will randomize.",
