        "wikipedia_extract": "Porphyry ( POR-fə-ree) is any of various granites or igneous rocks with coarse-grained crystals such as feldspar or quartz dispersed in a fine-grained silicate-rich, generally aphanitic matrix or groundmass. In its non-geologic, traditional use, the term porphyry usually refers to the purple-red form of this stone, valued for its appearance, but other colours of decorative porphyry are also used such as \"green\", \"black\" and \"grey\". The term porphyry is from the Ancient Greek πορφύρα (porphyra), meaning \"purple\". Purple was the colour of royalty, and the Roman \"imperial porphyry\" was a deep purple igneous rock with large crystals of plagioclase. Some authors claimed the rock was the hardest known in antiquity. Thus porphyry was prized for monuments and building projects in Imperial Rome and thereafter."
