        "note": "Refers to grammatical articles like 'a', 'an', 'the'.",
        "wikipedia_extract": "In natural language, a determiner, also called a determinative (abbreviated DET), is a word or affix that combines with a noun to express its reference. Examples in English include articles (the and a/an), demonstratives (this, that), possessive determiners (my, their), and quantifiers (many, both). Not all languages have determiners, and not all systems of grammatical description recognize them as a distinct category. The linguistics term \"determiner\" was coined by Leonard Bloomfield in 1933. Bloomfield observed that in English, nouns often require a qualifying word, such as an article or adjective. He proposed that such words belong to a distinct class which he called \"determiners\"."
