        "note": "While often colloquially referred to as 'molluscs' due to superficial similarity, brachiopods are a distinct phylum.",
        "wikidata_id": "Q178272",
        "wikidata_description": "phylum of marine invertebrate animals, having a dorsal and ventral shell",
        "wikipedia_extract": "Brachiopods (), phylum Brachiopoda, are a phylum of animals that have hard \"valves\" (shells) on the upper and lower surfaces, unlike the left and right arrangement in bivalve molluscs. Brachiopod valves are hinged at the rear end, while the front can be opened for feeding or closed for protection. Two major categories are traditionally recognized, articulate and inarticulate brachiopods. The word \"articulate\" is used to describe the tooth-and-groove structures of the valve-hinge which is present in the articulate group, and absent from the inarticulate group. This is the leading diagnostic skeletal feature, by which the two main groups can be readily distinguished as fossils. Articulate brachiopods have toothed hinges and simple, vertically oriented opening and closing muscles."
